me picture. This means that $sec⁡x$ is an *even* function, and we have\n\n$$\nsec⁡(-x)=sec⁡x.\n$$\n\nTherefore,\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}sec⁡(-a) & =sec⁡a \\\\  & =-8.\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186269132 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186176058 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "66a1733a-d7bb-5c86-a154-9e54422053a7" :knowledge-point/title "Utilizing the Oddness Property of the Tangent Function"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "378e1e66-9bf4-50e3-a7c1-262bf4e92781" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{4}{5}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186266624} {:answer/id #uuid "3ef10c28-e626-5937-ba80-feb997f872b4" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{5}{4}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186266707} {:answer/id #uuid "4b3e15ed-e7b6-5ea7-b9ed-17bf737d8274" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-\\frac{4}{5}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186266811} {:answer/id #uuid "c3857dc6-9f35-5def-8ee2-c64c55acc069" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-\\frac{5}{4}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186267889} {:answer/id #uuid "d30ef100-9315-5778-b153-4a1a0a22e9d8" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-\\frac{1}{5}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186268026}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "4b3e15ed-e7b6-5ea7-b9ed-17bf737d8274" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-\\frac{4}{5}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186266811} :answer-field/id #uuid "d4a81cb7-756d-567d-b8be-538028bb5ed2" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186268787}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "b5606ff1-4177-5d4b-892b-30b0ba862cf3" :question/math-academy-id "q-136639" :question/problem "If $tan⁡a=\\frac{4}{5},$ then $tan⁡(-a)=$" :question/worked-solution "First, let's recall the graph of $y=tan⁡x.$\n\n![](images/df/df1aba3f5ff980de82a74dde8170ad708e379caf2188873bcddc83177e87c7a6.png)\n\nOur graph has rotational symmetry of order $2$ about the origin. If we rotate the graph ${180}^{∘}$ around the origin, we get the same picture. This means that $tan⁡x$ is an *odd* function, and we have\n\n$$\ntan⁡(-x)=-tan⁡x.\n$$\n\nTherefore, \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}tan⁡(-a) & =-tan⁡a \\\\  & =-\\frac{4}{5}.\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186269219 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186100878 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "288e49b0-f706-54de-86b7-a17248fe2408" :knowledge-point/title "Calculating the Area of the Parallelogram Spanned by Two Non-Parallel Vectors"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "186ee2ac-da11-5516-b903-e3b5e9480103" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "6" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186266346} {:answer/id #uuid "303f5039-136f-57c4-95b6-e45427ec1075" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "10" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186266570} {:answer/id #uuid "3d1f355e-f850-51c9-9c2a-fb7bc5bbb357" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "12" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186266684} {:answer/id #uuid "92a8e1d3-8eda-5c2f-9bb2-70ec28082ba2" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "11" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186267452} {:answer/id #uuid "a686bd68-cab0-5c69-85ea-bad821967fe1" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "4" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186267650}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "303f5039-136f-57c4-95b6-e45427ec1075" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "10" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186266570} :answer-field/id #uuid "ad50c151-5c0e-566c-ac0d-79838b4014d4" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186268718}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "cad6730b-4b4a-5d91-acf2-b03ce7ce4d8e" :question/math-academy-id "q-13944" :question/problem "![](images/7a/7a70285258e584708a7296a2a008536dec9c97750b48e5fe0eb2f8cb7761bed1.png)\n\nCalculate the area of the parallelogram in the figure." :question/worked-solution "From the diagram, we get the vectors\n\n$$\n\\text{u}=⟨3,2⟩,\\,\\text{v}=⟨2,-2⟩.\n$$\n\nFirst, we construct a matrix where the columns are made up of the components of the corresponding vectors:\n\n$$\nM=[\\begin{aligned}3 & 2 \\\\ 2 & -2\\end{aligned}]\n$$\n\nThen, we find the determinant:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}det(M) & =|\\begin{aligned}3 & 2 \\\\ 2 & -2\\end{aligned}| \\\\  & =3\\cdot (-2)-2\\cdot 2 \\\\  & =-10\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe absolute value of $det(M)$ gives the area $A$ of the parallelogram:\n\n$$\nA=|-10\\,|=10\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186269232 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186100878 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "288e49b0-f706-54de-86b7-a17248fe2408" :knowledge-point/title "Calculating the Area of the Parallelogram Spanned by Two Non-Parallel Vectors"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "2d768a08-43a7-50f3-bc2c-9d0aec2d145d" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "38" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186266547} {:answer/id #uuid "49c440bd-ae95-5742-ba82-03be224e0b41" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "23" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186266798} {:answer/id #uuid "650a0407-08e2-55ea-afa8-98afe8bbc506" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "12" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186267039} {:answer/id #uuid "7b1ac682-0ef5-54bf-9790-851d28380cff" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "0" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186267230} {:answer/id #uuid "dba30746-1bcd-5e32-976d-2a26e890257c" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "52" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186268088}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "49c440bd-ae95-5742-ba82-03be224e0b41" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "23" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186266798} :answer-field/id #uuid "d0ea674a-8479-5833-a353-9cc54ecf9166" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186268781}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "19e02ca3-a6f7-5909-838c-8df1ebb7ea40" :question/math-academy-id "q-13951" :question/problem "Calculate the area of the parallelogram spanned by $\\text{u}=[\\begin{aligned}1 \\\\ -4\\end{aligned}]$ and $\\text{v}=[\\begin{aligned}5 \\\\ 3\\end{aligned}].$" :question/worked-solution "First, we construct a matrix where the columns are made up of the components of the corresponding vectors:\n\n$$\nM=[\\begin{aligned}1 & 5 \\\\ -4 & 3\\end{aligned}]\n$$\n\nThen, we find the determinant:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}det(M) & =|\\begin{aligned}1 & 5 \\\\ -4 & 3\\end{aligned}| \\\\  & =1\\cdot 3-5\\cdot (-4) \\\\  & =23\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe absolute value of $det(M)$ gives the area $A$ of the parallelogram:\n\n$$\nA=|\\,23\\,|=23\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186271964 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186087819 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "10ca3efc-7d23-5bc1-ab41-f785bed69986" :knowledge-point/title "Matrices for Route Planning"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "257b433d-960b-5602-9139-f0a08f725b10" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "4" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186269602} {:answer/id #uuid "2912ecc2-b82b-545b-a134-0f849b9f6f10" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "2" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186269638} {:answer/id #uuid "a28f67e7-b07f-5e99-99f1-6ef0ca27df71" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "0" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186270719} {:answer/id #uuid "ab496260-2eb5-5f24-b36a-b5c83e218fef" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "3" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186270799} {:answer/id #uuid "cee52816-3d9a-5a2d-bf35-0841387ab28c" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "1" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186271084}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "2912ecc2-b82b-545b-a134-0f849b9f6f10" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "2" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186269638} :answer-field/id #uuid "2a50b6d4-0ed8-59a9-bfe9-13d431937f58" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186271588}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "cf86f867-a1a5-56dc-89e8-e2255e71399b" :question/math-academy-id "q-139689" :question/problem "![](images/6c/6c52f315d079b82fb729c4ab47f210ec4bf2683d0dfe1adfe79c94db9fbc5f63.png)\n\nThe diagram above shows train routes between three stations, where nodes represent the stations and arrows represent the directed routes between them. The (incomplete) route matrix below represents information about the number of routes between each of the three stations. Find the value of $a+b+c.$\n\n|  |  | To: |  |  |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- | --- |\n|  |  | **A** | **B** | **C** |\n| From: | **A** | $1$ | $a$ | $0$ |\n| **B** | $0$ | $b$ | $2$ |  |\n| **C** | $c$ | $1$ | $0$ |  |" :question/worked-solution "Let's examine our diagram.\n\n- There is $1$ arrow starting at Station A and ending at Station B. So, there is only one route from $A$ to $B,$ and so $a=1.$\n\n- There is $1$ arrow starting at Station B and ending at Station B. So, there is only one route from $B$ to $B,$ and so $b=1.$\n\n- There are no arrows starting at Station C and ending at Station A. So, there are no routes from $C$ to $A,$ and so $c=0.$\n\nAs a result, our route matrix is the following:\n\n|  |  | To: |  |  |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- | --- |\n|  |  | **A** | **B** | **C** |\n| From: | **A** | $1$ | $1$ | $0$ |\n| **B** | $0$ | $1$ | $2$ |  |\n| **C** | $0$ | $1$ | $0$ |  |\n\nTherefore, \n\n$$\na+b+c=1+1+0=2.\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186060044 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186059851 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "09aeb7f5-31e0-5b2d-9e2a-8eace54a6665" :knowledge-point/title "Calculating the Cross Product of Two Vectors Whose Angle Is a Non-Special Angle"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "60742969-581c-5973-8fa9-78e77a0bf094" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "36n" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186270120} {:answer/id #uuid "622b545e-1af8-5ad8-944d-8f87481ebdc4" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "27n" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186270133} {:answer/id #uuid "7cbbc011-0ab5-5321-8b62-8396249479da" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "29n" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186270362} {:answer/id #uuid "84e582c4-e0ad-504a-9f91-14756ec4390b" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "43n" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186270417} {:answer/id #uuid "b177a505-8449-5d54-8746-4506cea92190" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "34n" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186270854}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "7cbbc011-0ab5-5321-8b62-8396249479da" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "29n" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186270362} :answer-field/id #uuid "ba0ffccc-6715-58cf-bae1-c7df2b05cefa" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186271847}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "6fb7d87a-bb90-5349-a9b4-58de72e66222" :question/math-academy-id "q-1400" :question/problem "The vector $a$ has magnitude $9,$ the vector $b$ has magnitude $5,$ the angle between $a$ and $b$ is ${140}^{∘}$ and $n$ is the unit vector that points in the direction of $a\\times b.$ Find $a\\times b$ in terms of $n.$ Round your final answer to the nearest integer where appropriate." :question/requires-calculator true :question/worked-solution "We use the formula for the cross product and obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}a\\times b & =|a|\\cdot |b|\\cdot \\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta \\cdot n \\\\  & =9\\cdot 5\\cdot \\operatorname{sin}⁡{140}^{∘}\\cdot n \\\\  & =45\\cdot (0.642…)\\cdot n \\\\  & =(28.925…)\\cdot n \\\\  & ≈29n.\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186272044 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186068874 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "bf9b812d-0dbf-5a67-9d45-3cc5e373b2b1" :knowledge-point/title "Completing the Square When the Perfect Square is Isolated: Negative Linear Term"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "1de2e53b-3e5a-515c-aeb7-08b2b37df9ba" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "(x-5{)}^{2}+1" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186269517} {:answer/id #uuid "2044f8ba-a526-5264-9c8b-279475bc6079" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "(x+5{)}^{2}+1" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186269538} {:answer/id #uuid "4ef60e4f-a72a-5357-acf3-4233e5a280e9" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "(x-25{)}^{2}+1" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186269981} {:answer/id #uuid "d1396c9b-3b30-5a34-9af3-bb9767985973" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "(x-10{)}^{2}+1" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186271110} {:answer/id #uuid "e97a70aa-76d8-5b9c-aa63-ecd9752ccf4f" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "(x+10{)}^{2}+1" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186271312}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "1de2e53b-3e5a-515c-aeb7-08b2b37df9ba" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "(x-5{)}^{2}+1" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186269517} :answer-field/id #uuid "501bcbfe-9b64-5b29-8ec4-43efe59a3a60" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186271656}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "0a5ade6b-92c1-5055-8b0b-c80cbaff9d2f" :question/math-academy-id "q-140281" :question/problem "Rewrite the expression $({x}^{2}-10x+25)+1$ as the sum of a perfect square and a constant." :question/worked-solution "First, we recall the following formula:\n\n$$\n{x}^{2}-2ax+{a}^{2}=(x-a{)}^{2}\n$$\n\nNotice that we have a perfect square in the parentheses that we can factor as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{x}^{2}-10x+25 & = \\\\ {x}^{2}-10x+{5}^{2} & = \\\\ {x}^{2}-2\\cdot 5x+{5}^{2} & = \\\\ (x-5{)}^{2} & \\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}({x}^{2}-10x+25)+1 & = \\\\ (x-5{)}^{2}+1 & .\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186272045 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186068874 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "bf9b812d-0dbf-5a67-9d45-3cc5e373b2b1" :knowledge-point/title "Completing the Square When the Perfect Square is Isolated: Negative Linear Term"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "098366e9-6bbf-557f-8b0b-c28f1f5c5d06" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "(x+12{)}^{2}-4" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186269356} {:answer/id #uuid "229c01ab-0ea9-5605-90a1-1d5a7751e77a" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "(x-11{)}^{2}-4" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186269568} {:answer/id #uuid "9d389434-bcbe-5f2b-ab97-000df772d7d9" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "(x-12{)}^{2}-4" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186270659} {:answer/id #uuid "aa8a2c46-7cb2-59b0-9203-35410fcdd6dd" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "(x-22{)}^{2}-4" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186270793} {:answer/id #uuid "b0f56343-9654-51c6-8939-85100071b4dd" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "(x+11{)}^{2}-4" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186270849}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "229c01ab-0ea9-5605-90a1-1d5a7751e77a" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "(x-11{)}^{2}-4" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186269568} :answer-field/id #uuid "90eb344c-2847-58a2-8769-e020f945532e" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186271768}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "ff51e775-e2b4-585e-a683-f90782853abe" :question/math-academy-id "q-140283" :question/problem "Rewrite the expression $({x}^{2}-22x+121)-4$ as the difference between a perfect square and a constant." :question/worked-solution "First, we recall the following formula:\n\n$$\n{x}^{2}-2ax+{a}^{2}=(x-a{)}^{2}\n$$\n\nNotice that we have a perfect square in the parentheses that we can factor as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{x}^{2}-22x+121 & = \\\\ {x}^{2}-22x+{11}^{2} & = \\\\ {x}^{2}-2\\cdot 11x+{11}^{2} & = \\\\ (x-11{)}^{2} & \\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}({x}^{2}-22x+121)-4 & = \\\\ (x-11{)}^{2}-4 & .\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186272058 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186184322 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "2a85fece-e40f-543c-b3c3-ae43a5175808" :knowledge-point/title "Completing the Square When the Leading Coefficient is Negative One: Odd Linear Terms"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "071c98e7-f045-5699-97e6-8ca98ff4c425" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{89}{4}-{(x+14)}^{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186269336} {:answer/id #uuid "0b6551d7-ff9d-5fcb-9ba8-47eb76d9737a" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{9}{4}-{(x+\\frac{7}{2})}^{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186269367} {:answer/id #uuid "92214c5a-0fb3-5f9c-b2fd-8c3a954013bf" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "59-{(x+14)}^{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186270546} {:answer/id #uuid "b1f6b651-8575-5bc8-a027-fe8e7ce11c40" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{7}{2}-{(x+\\frac{7}{2})}^{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186270861} {:answer/id #uuid "d730e81c-c1fd-5d3a-9ed2-255c3dad533b" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{49}{4}-{(x+\\frac{7}{2})}^{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186271160}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "0b6551d7-ff9d-5fcb-9ba8-47eb76d9737a" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{9}{4}-{(x+\\frac{7}{2})}^{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186269367} :answer-field/id #uuid "9e101ba8-4e79-5217-9a66-13f1e2c2b19e" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186271788}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "5f049fbc-7296-56e3-bb5f-3d51d2204d7a" :question/math-academy-id "q-140330" :question/problem "Rewrite $-({x}^{2}+7x+10)$ by completing the square." :question/worked-solution "We begin by completing the square for the expression inside the parentheses:\n\n$$\n{x}^{2}+7x+10\n$$\n\nFirst, we rewrite the coefficient of the linear term as a product with $2$ as a factor:\n\n$$\n{x}^{2}+2\\cdot \\frac{7}{2}x+10\n$$\n\nThen, we *add* and *then subtract* ${(\\frac{7}{2})}^{2},$ writing the constant term $10$ last:\n\n$$\n{{x}^{2}+2\\cdot \\frac{7}{2}x+{(\\frac{7}{2})}^{2}}_{⏟}-{(\\frac{7}{2})}^{2}+10\n$$\n\nThe first three terms form a perfect square, which we factor as follows:\n\n$$\n{(x+\\frac{7}{2})}^{2}-{(\\frac{7}{2})}^{2}+10\n$$\n\nEvaluating the last two terms, we get\n\n$$\n{(x+\\frac{7}{2})}^{2}-\\frac{9}{4}.\n$$\n\nFinally, we substitute this result back into our original expression:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}-({x}^{2}+7x+10) & =-({(x+\\frac{7}{2})}^{2}-\\frac{9}{4}) \\\\  & =-{(x+\\frac{7}{2})}^{2}+\\frac{9}{4} \\\\  & =\\frac{9}{4}-{(x+\\frac{7}{2})}^{2}.\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186272060 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186184322 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "2a85fece-e40f-543c-b3c3-ae43a5175808" :knowledge-point/title "Completing the Square When the Leading Coefficient is Negative One: Odd Linear Terms"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "2e0b9d03-98ee-5f65-8899-eb0ca3293f69" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{1}{4}-{(y+\\frac{9}{2})}^{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186269682} {:answer/id #uuid "51675449-e3ed-5d32-a5a1-dc181d745b38" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{161}{4}-{(y-\\frac{9}{2})}^{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186269997} {:answer/id #uuid "64001e55-d244-54a8-918a-a1c0059b44a6" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{3}{4}-{(y+\\frac{9}{2})}^{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186270154} {:answer/id #uuid "88d86ace-1240-59e6-93cf-c53adca2f146" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "61-{(y+18)}^{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186270450} {:answer/id #uuid "e002a466-5bda-58b6-b497-ea8df55b4790" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{9}{4}-{(y+\\frac{9}{2})}^{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186271234}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "2e0b9d03-98ee-5f65-8899-eb0ca3293f69" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{1}{4}-{(y+\\frac{9}{2})}^{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186269682} :answer-field/id #uuid "19597342-b978-5e00-8333-b2a8755a3138" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186271554}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "6187e130-749f-5a82-add1-d28e2491c294" :question/math-academy-id "q-140333" :question/problem "Rewrite $-({y}^{2}+9y+20)$ by completing the square." :question/worked-solution "We begin by completing the square for the expression inside the parentheses:\n\n$$\n{y}^{2}+9y+20\n$$\n\nFirst, we rewrite the coefficient of the linear term as a product with $2$ as a factor:\n\n$$\n{y}^{2}+2\\cdot \\frac{9}{2}y+20\n$$\n\nThen, we *add* and *then subtract* ${(\\frac{9}{2})}^{2},$ writing the constant term $20$ last:\n\n$$\n{{y}^{2}+2\\cdot \\frac{9}{2}y+{(\\frac{9}{2})}^{2}}_{⏟}-{(\\frac{9}{2})}^{2}+20\n$$\n\nThe first three terms form a perfect square, which we factor as follows:\n\n$$\n{(y+\\frac{9}{2})}^{2}-{(\\frac{9}{2})}^{2}+20\n$$\n\nEvaluating the last two terms, we get\n\n$$\n{(y+\\frac{9}{2})}^{2}-\\frac{1}{4}.\n$$\n\nFinally, we substitute this result back into our original expression:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}-({y}^{2}+9y+20) & =-({(y+\\frac{9}{2})}^{2}-\\frac{1}{4}) \\\\  & =-{(y+\\frac{9}{2})}^{2}+\\frac{1}{4} \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{4}-{(y+\\frac{9}{2})}^{2}.\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186184484 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186184212 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "3202d7cc-541b-553b-8ec1-d4ea85dd0590" :knowledge-point/title "Completing the Square on an Expression With a Negative Leading Coefficient"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "2441e55c-3656-5b9c-9444-a562a3ff3b87" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-9{(z-\\frac{1}{3})}^{2}+10" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186269586} {:answer/id #uuid "4f3faa2d-7189-540c-8002-572e91da81bc" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-9{(z+\\frac{1}{3})}^{2}+5" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186269984} {:answer/id #uuid "82317f98-eb55-5a2a-84b7-c0fc441aa39a" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-9{(z-\\frac{1}{3})}^{2}-8" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186270394} {:answer/id #uuid "ca440db9-063c-5ec8-89e2-7e47d8b1e800" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-9{(z+\\frac{1}{3})}^{2}+10" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186271044} {:answer/id #uuid "f37d3db7-779c-579c-a14f-a3675bd5b658" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-9{(z+\\frac{1}{3})}^{2}+8" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186271404}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "ca440db9-063c-5ec8-89e2-7e47d8b1e800" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-9{(z+\\frac{1}{3})}^{2}+10" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186271044} :answer-field/id #uuid "02be3ebb-ff12-5196-b096-96eac16ef254" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186271509}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045486 :db/ident :question.difficulty/hard} :question/id #uuid "2495fc0b-8e76-56e1-bf72-c223c52c4fc2" :question/math-academy-id "q-140378" :question/problem "Rewrite $-9{z}^{2}-6z+9$ by completing the square." :question/worked-solution "Notice that the leading coefficient is $-9.$ So, we take out a factor of $-9$ from the entire expression:\n\n$$\n-9({z}^{2}+\\frac{2}{3}z-1)\n$$\n\nLet's now complete the square for the expression inside the parentheses:\n\n$$\n{z}^{2}+\\frac{2}{3}z-1\n$$\n\nFirst, we rewrite the coefficient of the linear term as a product with $2$ as a factor:\n\n$$\n{z}^{2}+2\\cdot \\frac{1}{3}\\cdot z-1\n$$\n\nThen, we *add* and *then subtract* ${(\\frac{1}{3})}^{2},$ writing the constant term $1$ last:\n\n$$\n{{z}^{2}+2\\cdot \\frac{1}{3}\\cdot z+{(\\frac{1}{3})}^{2}}_{⏟}-{(\\frac{1}{3})}^{2}-1\n$$\n\nThe first three terms form a perfect square, which we factor as follows:\n\n$$\n{(z+\\frac{1}{3})}^{2}-{(\\frac{1}{3})}^{2}-1\n$$\n\nEvaluating the last two terms, we get\n\n$$\n{(z+\\frac{1}{3})}^{2}-\\frac{10}{9}.\n$$\n\nFinally, we substitute this result back into our original expression::\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}-9({z}^{2}+\\frac{2}{3}z-1) & =-9({(z+\\frac{1}{3})}^{2}-\\frac{10}{9}) \\\\  & =-9{(z+\\frac{1}{3})}^{2}+(-9)\\cdot (-\\frac{10}{9}) \\\\  & =-9{(z+\\frac{1}{3})}^{2}+10\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186272081 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186184322 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "2a85fece-e40f-543c-b3c3-ae43a5175808" :knowledge-point/title "Completing the Square When the Leading Coefficient is Negative One: Odd Linear Terms"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "4d854445-4b9d-5457-8fdd-3e9aafaf84c7" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{45}{4}-{(y-10)}^{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186269970} {:answer/id #uuid "6c18b098-7360-5163-a283-eff16352c701" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{25}{4}-{(y-\\frac{5}{2})}^{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186270231} {:answer/id #uuid "89f2f572-b7c1-5dea-a169-978a09aab668" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-\\frac{5}{2}+{(y-\\frac{5}{2})}^{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186270461} {:answer/id #uuid "aa2bcef6-9c81-5bea-950e-d8b1ddb76848" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "20-{(y-10)}^{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186270787} {:answer/id #uuid "ed555ea6-dfea-51d4-876e-75d752688105" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{5}{4}-{(y-\\frac{5}{2})}^{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186271354}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "ed555ea6-dfea-51d4-876e-75d752688105" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{5}{4}-{(y-\\frac{5}{2})}^{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186271354} :answer-field/id #uuid "880bee47-a8c3-50a6-937c-697f411c6825" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186271749}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "afc8b4da-a3f1-5590-a7d5-e7a094b395bd" :question/math-academy-id "q-140410" :question/problem "Rewrite $-{y}^{2}+5y-5$ by completing the square." :question/worked-solution "Notice that the leading coefficient is $-1.$ So, we take out a factor of $-1$ from the entire expression:\n\n$$\n-({y}^{2}-5y+5)\n$$\n\nLet's now complete the square for the expression inside the parentheses:\n\n$$\n{y}^{2}-5y+5\n$$\n\nFirst, we rewrite the coefficient of the linear term as a product with $2$ as a factor:\n\n$$\n{y}^{2}-2\\cdot \\frac{5}{2}y+5\n$$\n\nThen, we *add* and *then subtract* ${(\\frac{5}{2})}^{2},$ writing the constant term $5$ last:\n\n$$\n{{y}^{2}-2\\cdot \\frac{5}{2}y+{(\\frac{5}{2})}^{2}}_{⏟}-{(\\frac{5}{2})}^{2}+5\n$$\n\nThe first three terms form a perfect square, which we factor as follows:\n\n$$\n{(y-\\frac{5}{2})}^{2}-{(\\frac{5}{2})}^{2}+5\n$$\n\nEvaluating the last two terms, we get\n\n$$\n{(y-\\frac{5}{2})}^{2}-\\frac{5}{4}.\n$$\n\nFinally, we substitute this result back into our original expression:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}-{y}^{2}+5y-5 & =-({(y-\\frac{5}{2})}^{2}-\\frac{5}{4}) \\\\  & =\\frac{5}{4}-{(y-\\frac{5}{2})}^{2}.\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186272085 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186096611 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "8b7d0153-6e0a-5e19-83f1-960811dc5205" :knowledge-point/title "Differentiating the Product of a Constant and the Exponential Function, Plus a Polynomial"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "0884ad38-9d14-5f8e-b665-d8b35b5af237" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "e+4" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186269346} {:answer/id #uuid "0db0ac08-c4ce-5d3f-845f-d38358413506" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "e+8" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186269388} {:answer/id #uuid "2934301c-7684-5c1f-b910-d7819431eba3" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "2e" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186269640} {:answer/id #uuid "7789397b-7630-5bd8-9c80-42e72f128cee" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "2e+8" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186270328} {:answer/id #uuid "81639cc6-e903-5cd2-bc14-50bfc72b4d70" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "2e+4" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186270388}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "7789397b-7630-5bd8-9c80-42e72f128cee" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "2e+8" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186270328} :answer-field/id #uuid "c7c2979c-8072-5209-9ca0-20ccbaf77003" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186271876}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "d2ffa4af-5dbe-569b-8d78-f233584a8913" :question/math-academy-id "q-140431" :question/problem "Given that $f(x)=2{e}^{x}+4{x}^{2}-3$, find the slope of the tangent at $x=1.$" :question/worked-solution "First, using the addition and the power rules, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{f}^{′}(x) & =\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}(2{e}^{x}+4{x}^{2}-3) \\\\  & =\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}(2{e}^{x})+\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}(4{x}^{2})+\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}(-3) \\\\  & =2\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}({e}^{x})+4\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}({x}^{2})+0 \\\\  & =2{e}^{x}+8x.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the slope of the tangent at $x=1$ is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\frac{\\text{d}f}{\\text{d}x}|}_{x=1} & ={(2{e}^{x}+8x)\\,|}_{x=1} \\\\  & =2\\cdot {e}^{1}+8\\cdot 1 \\\\  & =2e+8.\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186400909 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186129253 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "4d48e2a0-1a76-5eec-a98c-4a7eb16f70cb" :knowledge-point/title "Identifying Multiplicity of a Root Given a Graph"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "3559bdb9-c007-500b-83db-63f2d91cd407" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$x=-3$ is a multiple root of odd multiplicity" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186400911} {:answer/id #uuid "f1984a87-93e4-523e-80b4-a37c28b7dc5b" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$x=-3$ is a multiple root of even multiplicity" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186400912} {:answer/id #uuid "3f15fef9-83c3-50e8-980b-72c67ab596d8" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$x=-3$ is a simple root of even multiplicity" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186400913} {:answer/id #uuid "f52c0001-2df6-56e0-ae3c-2dca7d79a58b" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$x=-3$ is not a root of $p(x)$" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186400914} {:answer/id #uuid "d7950f6f-fd87-5dac-ba8c-650fe8a19888" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$x=-3$ is a simple root" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186400915}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "d7950f6f-fd87-5dac-ba8c-650fe8a19888" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$x=-3$ is a simple root" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186400915} :answer-field/id #uuid "5806f142-c711-5da8-b1d4-68c8001a3f1f" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186400910}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "6ab70a1a-e04b-524f-9866-e86f88144126" :question/math-academy-id "q-141140" :question/problem "![](images/b3/b362831ba2248e03537551c412ae7537646dcd09f0d00a598905f0d52a258da9.png)\n\nA part of the graph of a polynomial $p(x)$ is given above. Which of the following statements is true?" :question/worked-solution "| Multiplicity | Sketch | Sketch |\n| --- | --- | --- |\n| $1$ \n\n (Simple Root) | ![](images/5a/5a0221f93c65a1eeb4503604a558a6da81c4c7af81c2c52cc35ecf3d189fe99f.png) | ![](images/d5/d556b4edf39181eebb7ec87c344d6f657a6875ecd2745405eaf5351ccaff6f96.png) |\n| $>1$ \n\n (Multiple Root) \n\n Even | ![](images/9d/9d14c2588da2ee9c9f5bbc32a70791146bed609ea0d6d5912aeaa2edab02e2d7.png) | ![](images/28/28ad9248a9a509e3a44937d0438fb2366f550a22360d01b9b6a29bcac41357e8.png) |\n| $>1$ \n\n (Multiple Root) \n\n Odd | ![](images/3a/3ae81378cb3c918dd5b7730519eb5a91e949f88eef9f03f4910f4ab8fb3cd5a9.png) | ![](images/95/9506a7ce4031f1f6244c0110c3235f66ea5652bb53548a032732869cf21f5997.png) |\n\nIt's possible to determine information regarding the multiplicities of the roots of a polynomial from its graph.\n\n- If the graph intersects the $x$-axis but is *not* tangent to the $x$-axis at $x=a,$ we have a *simple* root $x=a.$\n\n- If the graph is *tangent* to the $x$-axis at $x=a,$ we have a *multiple* root $x=a.$ Furthermore:\n\n- If the graph touches the $x$-axis and reverses direction at $x=a,$ the multiplicity of a root $x=a$ is *even*.\n\n- If the graph crosses the $x$-axis at $x=a,$ the multiplicity of a root $x=a$ is *odd*.\n\nIn our case, the graph is *not* tangent to the $x$-axis at $x=-3.$ Therefore, $x=-3$ is a *simple* root."}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186272221 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186129253 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "4d48e2a0-1a76-5eec-a98c-4a7eb16f70cb" :knowledge-point/title "Identifying Multiplicity of a Root Given a Graph"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "7a762869-8c41-5b99-945a-904933273d75" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$x=6$ is a simple root" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186270354} {:answer/id #uuid "8e0792d1-d0d7-58d4-acd0-2928fe61d064" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$x=6$ is a multiple root of odd multiplicity" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186270498} {:answer/id #uuid "a19bd95b-bbd6-55a2-9a46-010b458abb4e" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$x=6$ is not a root of $p(x)$" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186270704} {:answer/id #uuid "c3b75751-2d73-5678-8386-167ce0d8f782" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$x=6$ is a simple root of even multiplicity" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186270997} {:answer/id #uuid "f84b8666-1809-58bd-bfe6-8ab9488eb61f" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$x=6$ is a multiple root of even multiplicity" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186271447}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "f84b8666-1809-58bd-bfe6-8ab9488eb61f" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$x=6$ is a multiple root of even multiplicity" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186271447} :answer-field/id #uuid "eb90200a-e2bb-5eaf-ba24-af1df789d82c" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186271925}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "86545112-71e2-5627-8c9c-5a320524ae07" :question/math-academy-id "q-141144" :question/problem "![](images/40/4092c0852a573a0add69c6fb7b55f393d0767a5b6a2282a10194ea27cb1be9c4.png)\n\nA part of the graph of a polynomial $p(x)$ is shown above. Which of the following statements is true?" :question/worked-solution "| Multiplicity | Sketch | Sketch |\n| --- | --- | --- |\n| $1$ \n\n (Simple Root) | ![](images/5a/5a0221f93c65a1eeb4503604a558a6da81c4c7af81c2c52cc35ecf3d189fe99f.png) | ![](images/d5/d556b4edf39181eebb7ec87c344d6f657a6875ecd2745405eaf5351ccaff6f96.png) |\n| $>1$ \n\n (Multiple Root) \n\n Even | ![](images/9d/9d14c2588da2ee9c9f5bbc32a70791146bed609ea0d6d5912aeaa2edab02e2d7.png) | ![](images/28/28ad9248a9a509e3a44937d0438fb2366f550a22360d01b9b6a29bcac41357e8.png) |\n| $>1$ \n\n (Multiple Root) \n\n Odd | ![](images/3a/3ae81378cb3c918dd5b7730519eb5a91e949f88eef9f03f4910f4ab8fb3cd5a9.png) | ![](images/95/9506a7ce4031f1f6244c0110c3235f66ea5652bb53548a032732869cf21f5997.png) |\n\nIt's possible to determine information regarding the multiplicities of the roots of a polynomial from its graph.\n\n- If the graph intersects the $x$-axis but is *not* tangent to the $x$-axis at $x=a,$ we have a *simple* root $x=a.$\n\n- If the graph is *tangent* to the $x$-axis at $x=a,$ we have a *multiple* root $x=a.$ Furthermore:\n\n- If the graph touches the $x$-axis and reverses direction at $x=a,$ the multiplicity of a root $x=a$ is *even*.\n\n- If the graph crosses the $x$-axis at $x=a,$ the multiplicity of a root $x=a$ is *odd*.\n\nIn our case, the graph is *tangent* to the $x$-axis at $x=6.$ So, $x=6$ is a *multiple* root. \n\nFurthermore, the graph touches the $x$-axis and reverses direction at $x=6.$ So, $x=6$ has *even* multiplicity.\n\nTherefore, $x=6$ is a multiple root of even multiplicity."}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186272254 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186059662 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "69e91ce7-259a-5a11-bb79-7e6ad7ea86d4" :knowledge-point/title "Simplifying Expressions in Degrees Using the Sum and Difference Formulas"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "12266a51-a7c9-5483-94da-be9664d1b481" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-\\frac{\\sqrt{2}(\\operatorname{sin}⁡x+\\operatorname{cos}⁡x)}{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186269429} {:answer/id #uuid "18eff7de-8c89-5305-88dc-9781c493dbed" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{\\sqrt{3}\\operatorname{sin}⁡x-\\operatorname{cos}⁡x}{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186269477} {:answer/id #uuid "9784d4b6-191f-5d3c-bfd0-0ff7a24f4c76" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-\\frac{\\operatorname{sin}⁡x+\\sqrt{3}\\operatorname{cos}⁡x}{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186270602} {:answer/id #uuid "b5aefcfb-4daa-5443-aaa4-83730b1b02a2" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{\\operatorname{sin}⁡x-\\sqrt{3}\\operatorname{cos}⁡x}{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186270882} {:answer/id #uuid "f3d99ef5-ddef-5389-82d4-95a6ce517307" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{\\sqrt{2}(\\operatorname{sin}⁡x+\\operatorname{cos}⁡x)}{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186271407}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "9784d4b6-191f-5d3c-bfd0-0ff7a24f4c76" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-\\frac{\\operatorname{sin}⁡x+\\sqrt{3}\\operatorname{cos}⁡x}{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186270602} :answer-field/id #uuid "a0f40e9a-9d94-55f1-89bf-5ae1a4030b7c" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186271801}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "b6e1f817-7c7e-51c8-bd09-e888759f9be3" :question/math-academy-id "q-141275" :question/problem "$\\operatorname{sin}⁡(x-{120}^{∘})=$" :question/worked-solution "Recall the difference formula for sine:\n\n$$\n\\operatorname{sin}⁡(u-v)=\\operatorname{sin}⁡u\\operatorname{cos}⁡v-\\operatorname{cos}⁡u\\operatorname{sin}⁡v\n$$\n\nApplying the difference formula for sine with $u=x$ and $v={120}^{∘},$ we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\operatorname{sin}⁡(x-{120}^{∘}) & =\\operatorname{sin}⁡x\\operatorname{cos}⁡{120}^{∘}-\\operatorname{cos}⁡x\\operatorname{sin}⁡{120}^{∘} \\\\  & =\\operatorname{sin}⁡x\\cdot (-\\frac{1}{2})-\\operatorname{cos}⁡x\\cdot (\\frac{\\sqrt{3}}{2}) \\\\  & =-\\frac{\\operatorname{sin}⁡x}{2}-\\frac{\\sqrt{3}\\operatorname{cos}⁡x}{2} \\\\  & =-\\frac{\\operatorname{sin}⁡x+\\sqrt{3}\\operatorname{cos}⁡x}{2}.\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186272256 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186059662 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "69e91ce7-259a-5a11-bb79-7e6ad7ea86d4" :knowledge-point/title "Simplifying Expressions in Degrees Using the Sum and Difference Formulas"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "012c6c54-c102-5db4-8d24-caf1dec68123" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-\\operatorname{cos}⁡x" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186269269} {:answer/id #uuid "55dd97d3-3e02-5362-84db-9281bfaf98c8" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\operatorname{cos}⁡x" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186270029} {:answer/id #uuid "60c3ef61-d3ea-5891-85a9-bc3230333d6c" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{\\sqrt{3}\\operatorname{cos}⁡x-\\operatorname{sin}⁡x}{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186270123} {:answer/id #uuid "7837e062-26e0-54eb-9c88-937b73a56868" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-\\frac{\\sqrt{2}(\\operatorname{sin}⁡x+\\operatorname{cos}⁡x)}{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186270334} {:answer/id #uuid "9974f52a-a24a-58ca-913f-99e237c9a5bc" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-\\operatorname{sin}⁡x" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186270618}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "9974f52a-a24a-58ca-913f-99e237c9a5bc" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-\\operatorname{sin}⁡x" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186270618} :answer-field/id #uuid "bea83195-85b8-5a62-ae80-a9df9097b16d" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186271856}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "dd3a924d-681c-501c-bf69-7fbcdc5b6418" :question/math-academy-id "q-141278" :question/problem "$\\operatorname{sin}⁡(x-{180}^{∘})=$" :question/worked-solution "Recall the difference formula for sine:\n\n$$\n\\operatorname{sin}⁡(u-v)=\\operatorname{sin}⁡u\\operatorname{cos}⁡v-\\operatorname{cos}⁡u\\operatorname{sin}⁡v\n$$\n\nApplying the difference formula for sine with $u=x$ and $v={180}^{∘},$ we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\operatorname{sin}⁡(x-{180}^{∘}) & =\\operatorname{sin}⁡x\\operatorname{cos}⁡{180}^{∘}-\\operatorname{cos}⁡x\\operatorname{sin}⁡{180}^{∘} \\\\  & =\\operatorname{sin}⁡x\\cdot (-1)-\\operatorname{cos}⁡x\\cdot (0) \\\\  & =-\\operatorname{sin}⁡x.\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186272257 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186059820 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "4b12a7b7-1df3-5203-934e-8820604de3de" :knowledge-point/title "Simplifying Expressions in Radians Using the Sum and Difference Formulas"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "0da8bceb-5536-5271-8827-e5da61eb6060" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-\\operatorname{sin}⁡x" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186269387} {:answer/id #uuid "3ef50584-86f1-5b31-a1a6-a37093aa3120" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-\\operatorname{cos}⁡x" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186269846} {:answer/id #uuid "42fbd17c-de86-5394-814e-0ac710c28aed" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{\\sqrt{2}(\\operatorname{sin}⁡x-\\operatorname{cos}⁡x)}{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186269872} {:answer/id #uuid "ced45233-a48f-5d9c-bc03-580cf170e7ca" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-\\frac{\\sqrt{3}\\operatorname{sin}⁡x+\\operatorname{cos}⁡x}{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186271083} {:answer/id #uuid "d6ebddae-329f-55c0-89d1-c5bb4c99e094" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\operatorname{cos}⁡x" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186271157}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "3ef50584-86f1-5b31-a1a6-a37093aa3120" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-\\operatorname{cos}⁡x" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186269846} :answer-field/id #uuid "e714385a-3268-5f76-bbf9-458125ace391" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186271919}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "78f7a073-b897-5930-bd54-7d5e600e42be" :question/math-academy-id "q-141284" :question/problem "$\\operatorname{sin}⁡(x-\\frac{\\pi }{2})=$" :question/worked-solution "Recall the difference formula for sine:\n\n$$\n\\operatorname{sin}⁡(u-v)=\\operatorname{sin}⁡u\\operatorname{cos}⁡v-\\operatorname{cos}⁡u\\operatorname{sin}⁡v\n$$\n\nApplying the difference formula for sine with $u=x$ and $v=\\frac{\\pi }{2},$ we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\operatorname{sin}⁡(x-\\frac{\\pi }{2}) & =\\operatorname{sin}⁡x\\operatorname{cos}⁡(\\frac{\\pi }{2})-\\operatorname{cos}⁡x\\operatorname{sin}⁡(\\frac{\\pi }{2}) \\\\  & =\\operatorname{sin}⁡x\\cdot (0)-\\operatorname{cos}⁡x\\cdot (1) \\\\  & =-\\operatorname{cos}⁡x.\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186272270 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186059821 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "c6a58962-cb06-519a-8c64-0dfa3b9c14d7" :knowledge-point/title "Simplifying Expressions in Degrees Using the Sum and Difference Formulas"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "25783b4f-f3f1-5cc2-84df-24e666b31d94" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{\\sqrt{2}(\\operatorname{cos}⁡x+\\operatorname{sin}⁡x)}{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186269600} {:answer/id #uuid "6ffdb6d7-6d98-5836-9d05-4da7a12f0aac" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{\\sqrt{3}\\operatorname{cos}⁡x+\\operatorname{sin}⁡x}{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186270259} {:answer/id #uuid "84074aaa-f06b-558f-bcf0-aa133aa7738a" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{\\sqrt{3}\\operatorname{cos}⁡x-\\operatorname{sin}⁡x}{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186270409} {:answer/id #uuid "c08bd8f7-a322-5e2b-8ce7-5315830bd7b1" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{\\sqrt{2}(\\operatorname{cos}⁡x-\\operatorname{sin}⁡x)}{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186270974} {:answer/id #uuid "fc967a21-6b7e-51a5-8070-4d6aaad3f36a" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{\\operatorname{cos}⁡x-\\sqrt{3}\\operatorname{sin}⁡x}{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186271480}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "84074aaa-f06b-558f-bcf0-aa133aa7738a" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{\\sqrt{3}\\operatorname{cos}⁡x-\\operatorname{sin}⁡x}{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186270409} :answer-field/id #uuid "4424e579-c28d-53af-bbb7-f1ff4f62f6c8" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186271634}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "b5eb1983-1627-51b5-a21b-a1c11d629a2e" :question/math-academy-id "q-141364" :question/problem "$\\operatorname{cos}⁡(x+{30}^{∘})=$" :question/worked-solution "First, we recall the sum formula for cosine:\n\n$$\n\\operatorname{cos}⁡(u+v)=\\operatorname{cos}⁡u\\operatorname{cos}⁡v-\\operatorname{sin}⁡u\\operatorname{sin}⁡v\n$$\n\nApplying the sum formula for cosine with $u=x$ and $v={30}^{∘}$, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\operatorname{cos}⁡(x+{30}^{∘}) & =\\operatorname{cos}⁡x\\operatorname{cos}⁡{30}^{∘}-\\operatorname{sin}⁡x\\operatorname{sin}⁡{30}^{∘} \\\\  & =\\operatorname{cos}⁡x\\cdot \\frac{\\sqrt{3}}{2}-\\operatorname{sin}⁡x\\cdot \\frac{1}{2} \\\\  & =\\frac{\\sqrt{3}\\operatorname{cos}⁡x}{2}-\\frac{\\operatorname{sin}⁡x}{2} \\\\  & =\\frac{\\sqrt{3}\\operatorname{cos}⁡x-\\operatorname{sin}⁡x}{2}.\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186272275 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186059665 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "831f9aa0-099e-5e0c-8a9d-c4828b629f64" :knowledge-point/title "Simplifying Expressions in Radians Using the Sum and Difference Formulas"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "0b931fa1-ee52-5db3-bac9-497efc06f498" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-\\frac{\\sqrt{2}(\\operatorname{cos}⁡x+\\operatorname{sin}⁡x)}{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186269370} {:answer/id #uuid "2342b124-c9e0-58c0-a580-8d4f8695808a" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{\\sqrt{2}(\\operatorname{sin}⁡x-\\operatorname{cos}⁡x)}{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186269577} {:answer/id #uuid "69b15b7b-0cac-5374-93f1-818c8990768a" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{\\sqrt{3}\\operatorname{cos}⁡x+\\operatorname{sin}⁡x}{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186270207} {:answer/id #uuid "a358caaa-5c57-5746-b54c-5548bfb79b9b" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{\\operatorname{cos}⁡x-\\sqrt{3}\\operatorname{sin}⁡x}{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186270725} {:answer/id #uuid "dff6ea96-0ee2-5bc4-80b8-7b9f1617a512" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{\\operatorname{sin}⁡x-\\sqrt{3}\\operatorname{cos}⁡x}{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186271232}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "0b931fa1-ee52-5db3-bac9-497efc06f498" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-\\frac{\\sqrt{2}(\\operatorname{cos}⁡x+\\operatorname{sin}⁡x)}{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186269370} :answer-field/id #uuid "3f2bd73b-78d1-5e9e-92ad-582b63f4ea0e" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186271623}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045486 :db/ident :question.difficulty/hard} :question/id #uuid "308956a9-f5f0-59d1-8748-67ba18adb1f8" :question/math-academy-id "q-141381" :question/problem "$\\operatorname{cos}⁡(x+\\frac{3\\pi }{4})=$" :question/worked-solution "First, we recall the sum formula for cosine:\n\n$$\n\\operatorname{cos}⁡(u+v)=\\operatorname{cos}⁡u\\operatorname{cos}⁡v-\\operatorname{sin}⁡u\\operatorname{sin}⁡v\n$$\n\nApplying the sum formula for cosine with $u=x$ and $v=\\frac{3\\pi }{4},$ we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\operatorname{cos}⁡(x+\\frac{3\\pi }{4}) & =\\operatorname{cos}⁡x\\operatorname{cos}⁡(\\frac{3\\pi }{4})-\\operatorname{sin}⁡x\\operatorname{sin}⁡(\\frac{3\\pi }{4}) \\\\  & =\\operatorname{cos}⁡x\\cdot (-\\frac{\\sqrt{2}}{2})-\\operatorname{sin}⁡x\\cdot \\frac{\\sqrt{2}}{2} \\\\  & =-\\frac{\\sqrt{2}\\operatorname{cos}⁡x}{2}-\\frac{\\sqrt{2}\\operatorname{sin}⁡x}{2} \\\\  & =-\\frac{\\sqrt{2}(\\operatorname{cos}⁡x+\\operatorname{sin}⁡x)}{2}.\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186272277 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186059665 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "831f9aa0-099e-5e0c-8a9d-c4828b629f64" :knowledge-point/title "Simplifying Expressions in Radians Using the Sum and Difference Formulas"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "2a22d57d-9107-5ef5-9e1a-664d1e481595" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{\\operatorname{cos}⁡x+\\sqrt{3}\\operatorname{sin}⁡x}{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186269651} {:answer/id #uuid "2e7bf421-a5a6-51a7-bbde-dcaec6ed4724" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{\\sqrt{3}\\operatorname{cos}⁡x-\\operatorname{sin}⁡x}{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186269688} {:answer/id #uuid "8a0c5476-651b-5c29-aaf7-9c8b4495a59a" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{\\sqrt{2}(\\operatorname{cos}⁡x+\\operatorname{sin}⁡x)}{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186270462} {:answer/id #uuid "8a8eb468-d843-5e6f-a576-53b1846a95f7" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{\\operatorname{cos}⁡x-\\sqrt{3}\\operatorname{sin}⁡x}{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186270466} {:answer/id #uuid "ca8ff2c0-77a5-54e0-af66-4eb8ca206ccd" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{\\sqrt{2}(\\operatorname{cos}⁡x-\\operatorname{sin}⁡x)}{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186271048}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "2a22d57d-9107-5ef5-9e1a-664d1e481595" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{\\operatorname{cos}⁡x+\\sqrt{3}\\operatorname{sin}⁡x}{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186269651} :answer-field/id #uuid "64d9c7cb-8c90-5531-a052-3b459d3163b8" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186271688}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045486 :db/ident :question.difficulty/hard} :question/id #uuid "bedf2d85-2b35-59e9-bfbf-70576d25817c" :question/math-academy-id "q-141386" :question/problem "$\\operatorname{cos}⁡(x-\\frac{\\pi }{3})=$" :question/worked-solution "First, we recall  the difference formula for cosine:\n\n$$\n\\operatorname{cos}⁡(u-v)=\\operatorname{cos}⁡u\\operatorname{cos}⁡v+\\operatorname{sin}⁡u\\operatorname{sin}⁡v\n$$\n\nApplying the difference formula for cosine with $u=x$ and $v=\\frac{\\pi }{3},$ we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\operatorname{cos}⁡(x-\\frac{\\pi }{3}) & =\\operatorname{cos}⁡x\\operatorname{cos}⁡(\\frac{\\pi }{3})+\\operatorname{sin}⁡x\\operatorname{sin}⁡(\\frac{\\pi }{3}) \\\\  & =\\operatorname{cos}⁡x\\cdot \\frac{1}{2}+\\operatorname{sin}⁡x\\cdot \\frac{\\sqrt{3}}{2} \\\\  & =\\frac{\\operatorname{cos}⁡x}{2}+\\frac{\\sqrt{3}\\operatorname{sin}⁡x}{2} \\\\  & =\\frac{\\operatorname{cos}⁡x+\\sqrt{3}\\operatorname{sin}⁡x}{2}.\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186083535 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186083232 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "1b5f9ee9-a489-5048-8346-0116a19e9b4a" :knowledge-point/title "Counting the Number of Possible Rational Roots of a Polynomial"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "311f9529-efb3-578e-8744-809e1101f04a" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "10" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186269712} {:answer/id #uuid "6d3730b5-4247-558a-bff6-c3d95ce3ec3e" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "6" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186270236} {:answer/id #uuid "83f3968d-ef3f-53f0-ba78-c6e9d8fb524c" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "16" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186270408} {:answer/id #uuid "86ca815b-5e6f-55e6-91c6-bd46a039a85f" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "8" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186270438} {:answer/id #uuid "b1319d66-f133-598f-a450-27c576b92f22" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "20" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186270851}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "b1319d66-f133-598f-a450-27c576b92f22" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "20" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186270851} :answer-field/id #uuid "9ca9f1ac-2135-555e-8f76-360ce0db72a9" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186271787}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045486 :db/ident :question.difficulty/hard} :question/id #uuid "aeb1ad5f-0910-5dea-b64a-e03889cc2cc5" :question/math-academy-id "q-14145" :question/problem "For the polynomial $f(x)=10{x}^{6}-52{x}^{2}+50x-8,$ how many *potential* roots are given by the rational roots theorem?" :question/worked-solution "Given a polynomial with integer coefficients, the rational roots theorem states that any rational roots of the polynomial must take the form\n\n$$\n\\pm \\frac{\\text{factor of the constant term}}{\\text{factor of the leading coefficient}}.\n$$\n\nFor the given polynomial:\n\n- The constant term is $-8,$ and its factors are $\\pm 1,$ $\\pm 2,$ $\\pm 4,$ and $\\pm 8.$\n\n- The leading coefficient is $10,$ and its factors are $\\pm 1,$ $\\pm 2,$ $\\pm 5,$ and $\\pm 10.$\n\nFirst, we list out all the possibilities for the numerator and denominator:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\pm \\frac{1}{1}, & \\pm \\frac{1}{2}, & \\pm \\frac{1}{5}, & \\pm \\frac{1}{10}, \\\\ \\pm \\frac{2}{1}, & \\pm \\frac{2}{2}, & \\pm \\frac{2}{5}, & \\pm \\frac{2}{10}, \\\\ \\pm \\frac{4}{1}, & \\pm \\frac{4}{2}, & \\pm \\frac{4}{5}, & \\pm \\frac{4}{10}, \\\\ \\pm \\frac{8}{1}, & \\pm \\frac{8}{2}, & \\pm \\frac{8}{5}, & \\pm \\frac{8}{10}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNext, we reduce the fractions to lowest terms:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\pm 1, & \\pm \\frac{1}{2}, & \\pm \\frac{1}{5}, & \\pm \\frac{1}{10}, \\\\ \\pm 2, & \\pm 1, & \\pm \\frac{2}{5}, & \\pm \\frac{1}{5}, \\\\ \\pm 4, & \\pm 2, & \\pm \\frac{4}{5}, & \\pm \\frac{2}{5}, \\\\ \\pm 8, & \\pm 4, & \\pm \\frac{8}{5}, & \\pm \\frac{4}{5}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNotice that several potential roots are duplicates (shown above in red). Removing these duplicates, and listing those remaining in order from least to greatest in absolute value, we have\n\n$$\n\\pm \\frac{1}{10},\\,\\pm \\frac{1}{5},\\,\\pm \\frac{2}{5},\\,\\pm \\frac{1}{2},\\,\\pm \\frac{4}{5},\\,\\pm 1,\\,\\pm \\frac{8}{5},\\,\\pm 2,\\,\\pm 4,\\,\\pm 8.\n$$\n\nThis makes a total of $20$ potential roots. (Remember that each $\\pm $ sign accounts for $2$ roots, and $2\\cdot 10=20.$)"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186272292 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186068873 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "f10e4a34-a512-5ff4-abbc-66e6830d63d0" :knowledge-point/title "Completing the Square When the Perfect Square is Isolated"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "110ee8cd-e830-5bb3-bab0-f16cb26fe2b7" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "(y+4{)}^{2}-3" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186269421} {:answer/id #uuid "13e31713-81cb-5dec-8652-93d586dd34fb" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "(y-3{)}^{2}-3" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186269445} {:answer/id #uuid "29d7d2be-f02c-521d-b752-bf360c856fdf" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "(y+3{)}^{2}-3" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186269648} {:answer/id #uuid "5eb20cc7-de16-5bcf-8e1b-43826770ca5f" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "(y-6{)}^{2}-3" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186270109} {:answer/id #uuid "f84d763b-739b-535d-8995-04b2349046b0" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "(y+6{)}^{2}-3" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186271448}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "f84d763b-739b-535d-8995-04b2349046b0" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "(y+6{)}^{2}-3" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186271448} :answer-field/id #uuid "ee48d03a-569e-51f8-be78-887782d15f39" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186271927}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "48f5b61f-2f3f-5cda-a551-c1e861cdd220" :question/math-academy-id "q-141513" :question/problem "Rewrite the expression $({y}^{2}+12y+{6}^{2})-3$ as the difference between a perfect square and a constant." :question/worked-solution "First, we recall the following formula:\n\n$$\n{y}^{2}+2ay+{a}^{2}=(y+a{)}^{2}\n$$\n\nNotice that we have a perfect square in the parentheses that we can factor as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{y}^{2}+12y+{6}^{2} & = \\\\ {y}^{2}+2\\cdot 6y+{6}^{2} & = \\\\ (y+6{)}^{2} & \\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}({y}^{2}+12y+{6}^{2})-3 & = \\\\ (y+6{)}^{2}-3 & .\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186272294 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186184169 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "ec6076ff-1538-5bc0-973d-33d908b0b4d2" :knowledge-point/title "Completing the Square on a Quadratic Expression"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "31012e79-2696-578b-952b-a385f9bb0eb6" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{(y+\\frac{11}{2})}^{2}-\\frac{21}{4}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186269711} {:answer/id #uuid "80e62b24-0b58-586c-ab5e-10a92a99e5ed" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{(y+\\frac{11}{2})}^{2}-5" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186270386} {:answer/id #uuid "8e8982a4-a551-5a56-96cc-144410267cb0" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{(y+\\frac{11}{2})}^{2}-\\frac{25}{4}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186270506} {:answer/id #uuid "94a242c2-81bc-53a1-99fc-de5cfa4e26aa" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{(y+\\frac{11}{4})}^{2}-\\frac{21}{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186270571} {:answer/id #uuid "eaa96b06-cbed-595d-86c7-fdb048fe292f" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{(y+\\frac{11}{4})}^{2}-5" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186271324}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "31012e79-2696-578b-952b-a385f9bb0eb6" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{(y+\\frac{11}{2})}^{2}-\\frac{21}{4}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186269711} :answer-field/id #uuid "40dc7a0f-2584-5d4a-b845-07be3a13c2db" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186271628}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "b17fd33d-fef8-5118-8c44-b94f68ba1d49" :question/math-academy-id "q-141527" :question/problem "Complete the square on the quadratic expression ${y}^{2}+11y+25.$" :question/worked-solution "First, we rewrite the coefficient of the linear term as a product with $2$ as a factor:\n\n$$\n{y}^{2}+2\\cdot \\frac{11}{2}y+25\n$$\n\nNow, here's the trick. We *add* and *then subtract* ${(\\frac{11}{2})}^{2},$ writing the constant term $25$ last:\n\n$$\n{{y}^{2}+2\\cdot \\frac{11}{2}y+{(\\frac{11}{2})}^{2}}_{⏟}-{(\\frac{11}{2})}^{2}+25\n$$\n\nThe first three terms form a perfect square, which we factor as follows:\n\n$$\n{(y+\\frac{11}{2})}^{2}-{(\\frac{11}{2})}^{2}+25\n$$\n\nFinally, evaluating the last two terms, we get\n\n$$\n{(y+\\frac{11}{2})}^{2}-\\frac{21}{4}.\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186272295 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186184169 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "ec6076ff-1538-5bc0-973d-33d908b0b4d2" :knowledge-point/title "Completing the Square on a Quadratic Expression"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "20d0ce94-71f7-556b-a0d7-7b5e20af96e7" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{(y+\\frac{7}{2})}^{2}-\\frac{49}{4}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186269547} {:answer/id #uuid "5c56dfc4-4aef-5331-872d-da2b2274bae2" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{(y+\\frac{7}{2})}^{2}-\\frac{7}{4}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186270094} {:answer/id #uuid "bbd458c1-82ff-5e70-925e-2f5ec028580c" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{(y+\\frac{7}{2})}^{2}-7" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186270924} {:answer/id #uuid "ca96e8bc-27e6-5cce-ac58-a15424f3db5f" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{(y+\\frac{7}{4})}^{2}-7" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186271049} {:answer/id #uuid "e14d5b9e-029a-5589-8dcb-8d46977be99b" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{(y+\\frac{7}{4})}^{2}-\\frac{7}{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186271248}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "20d0ce94-71f7-556b-a0d7-7b5e20af96e7" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{(y+\\frac{7}{2})}^{2}-\\frac{49}{4}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186269547} :answer-field/id #uuid "4b5ddc0f-2757-59e8-9e14-a04b8fd5611f" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186271648}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "f6ca9c1a-eab9-55fb-98fa-ee3277281e1b" :question/math-academy-id "q-141536" :question/problem "Complete the square on the quadratic expression ${y}^{2}+7y.$" :question/worked-solution "First, we rewrite the coefficient of the linear term as a product with $2$ as a factor:\n\n$$\n{y}^{2}+2\\cdot \\frac{7}{2}y\n$$\n\nNow, here's the trick. We *add* and *then subtract* ${(\\frac{7}{2})}^{2}:$\n\n$$\n{{y}^{2}+2\\cdot \\frac{7}{2}y+{(\\frac{7}{2})}^{2}}_{⏟}-{(\\frac{7}{2})}^{2}\n$$\n\nThe first three terms form a perfect square, which we factor as follows:\n\n$$\n{(y+\\frac{7}{2})}^{2}-{(\\frac{7}{2})}^{2}\n$$\n\nFinally, evaluating the last term, we get\n\n$$\n{(y+\\frac{7}{2})}^{2}-\\frac{49}{4}.\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186272296 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186068875 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "366b36a5-c909-5ea5-a05d-a6052fcd41d7" :knowledge-point/title "Completing the Square on a Quadratic Expression"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "129c0ce9-31d0-501f-9253-202530581425" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "(x+5{)}^{2}+10" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186269432} {:answer/id #uuid "1e413b35-35e1-5ce6-a283-f4ff4b83d4f3" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "(x+10{)}^{2}-5" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186269522} {:answer/id #uuid "395d3e65-bf9a-53cf-a730-73e30942c7aa" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 1759